{"id":"safety-overtime-back-to-2015","tier":"REDFLAG","title":"Police, sheriff and fire overtime against other California employers, 2015 to 2025","question":"Was San Francisco’s police and jail overtime already high before 2019, or is the top ranking recent?","finding":"Police overtime was 14.3% of regular pay in 2015, rank 4 of 7 large-city police payrolls (middle of the others 15.0%); 18.2% in 2019 and 37.7% in 2025 (rank 1 of 7). Sheriff and jails: 20.2% in 2015, rank 2 of 13 (middle 16.0%), and 47.6% in 2025, rank 1 of 13. Fire: 23.6% in 2015, rank 12 of 14.","how":"State Controller raw exports: the 2015-2018 files saved on 20 November 2026 and the 2019-2025 files already used by the pay screens, read the same way. Departments are picked by the name each employer reports (police; sheriff, jail, detention, corrections or custody; fire), leaving out oversight, health and probation bodies. Peers are the seven largest other city payrolls and the largest county payrolls with the Bay Area counties, chosen in 2024 as in the pay screens. Overtime share is overtime divided by regular pay; employer-years with fewer than 50 paid positions are dropped.","limits":"Department names and what employers report as overtime differ; a department can be missed or civilian staff counted in. Vacancies, minimum staffing in labour agreements and events billed to others all raise overtime. The export does not say whether its years are calendar or fiscal years. A position is a row, not a person.","columns":[{"k":"service","t":"Service","type":"text"},{"k":"year","t":"Year","type":"year"},{"k":"sfShare","t":"San Francisco overtime as share of regular pay","type":"pct"},{"k":"peerMedian","t":"Middle of the others","type":"pct"},{"k":"rank","t":"San Francisco’s rank (1 = highest)","type":"int"},{"k":"compared","t":"Employers compared","type":"int"},{"k":"sfOvertime","t":"San Francisco overtime","type":"usd"},{"k":"sfPositions","t":"San Francisco paid positions","type":"int"},{"k":"outliers"